Inline the CPU's register accessors

The interpreter reaches the program counter, the general registers, HI/LO,
the stop flag and the coprocessor-0 registers through thirteen accessor
functions (r4300_pc_struct, r4300_regs, r4300_cp0_regs and the rest), each a
single `return &r4300->field;`. They were defined in r4300_core.c and cp0.c,
so every other file, the cached interpreter and the instruction definitions
included, called them as real functions: several calls per emulated
instruction, and after each one the compiler had to assume any memory could
have changed and reload what it held. They are now `static inline` in their
headers, bodies unchanged, so the compiler sees through them everywhere.

Nothing else changes: same bodies, no caller touched, no accessor's address
is taken anywhere. Measured with Tools/n64-bench on five homebrew games, 1,800
frames each: the picture and the sound identical on every frame, and the
emulation thread's CPU time per frame down from 5.6 to 2.3 ms and 5.1 to
2.1 ms on the two heaviest (host timings; link-time optimisation gives 2.0
and 1.8, but cannot run through this core's `ld -r` pre-link, see build.sh).

Applied at build time by Vendor/n64-ios/build.sh, after 0008.

